!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Flow
description: 'yaml/flow — one line, recorded and re-emitted'
- >
  `b: [12, 13, 14]` or `{x: 1, y: 2}` — the flow style: a JSON-looking value on one line inside a
  `.yo` file is simply a flow container; the language is still yamlover. Flow is recorded by the
  parser and re-emitted by the serializer, so an authored one-liner comes back a one-liner. Formatting
  is normalized, not preserved byte for byte: `[1,2]` and `[ 1, 2 ]` are both flow, and both re-emit
  as `[1, 2]` — the concrete is the classification, not the bytes. A json-family document is flow end
  to end by language and needs no per-node marker.
- >
  Flow cannot hold everything. A container carrying a path anchor, a `!!<…>` tag, a `!!yo` or `!!set`,
  a leading comment, a multi-line scalar, a blob, or a keyed+keyless mixture **falls back to block
  form** — silently and losslessly, never a throw and never a drop. The refusal list lives once, in
  the serializer, and the editor mirrors it, so the screen and the file agree.
- >
  A flow value is ONE source token, however long: an edit cannot address a path *into* one — the whole
  token is replaced instead.
